{%- comment -%}
  PG Chips: cart pill styling, and a SAVED % that tells the truth.

  (A) CENTRING. The FREE GIFT pill (pg-drawer's giftTag) and the SAVED n% pill
  (its pass()) are both stamped with INLINE `!important` styles the moment they
  are created: font-size:12px, line-height:1.5, padding:4px 12px. An inline
  !important declaration outranks every stylesheet rule, so this cannot be fixed
  in CSS -- the properties have to be re-set on the elements themselves.

  - display:inline-flex + centred alignment and line-height:1, so the glyphs sit
    in the middle of the pill instead of riding on a 1.5 line box.
  - text-indent matching the letter-spacing. Letter-spacing adds its gap AFTER
    the final letter too, which pushes the visual centre left; indenting by the
    same amount puts it back.
  - 12px -> 13px text and 4px 12px -> 7px 15px padding.

  (B) THE PERCENTAGE. pg-drawer works its SAVED n% out from compare-at against
  the variant's list price, which ignores every bundle discount actually applied
  to that line. A shopper who took the 3rd-orb-50%-off rung saw a line charged
  $16.50 against a $79.99 compare-at -- a real saving of 79% -- still badged
  SAVED 59%, the same as the undiscounted orb sitting above it. The badge was
  understating the offer precisely on the line the offer applied to, and two
  identical-looking lines carried identical badges at different prices.

  So the pill is recomputed from what the line is genuinely charged:

      saved% = 1 - line_price / (compare_at x quantity)

  line_price is cart.js's fully-discounted figure, so any BXGY rung, any
  combination of them now that the ladders stack, AND the automatic 10% order
  discount are all reflected. It has to be line_price rather than
  final_line_price: final_line_price counts line-level discounts only, so when
  the 10% became an order discount it silently dropped out and the pill started
  printing SAVED 50% beside a row reading $140.00 struck and $62.99 charged --
  figures that are 55% apart. compare_at comes from /products.json, which
  cart.js does not carry.

  (C) THE STRUCK PRICE. Same root cause, other half of the line. pg-drawer
  strikes through the variant's LIST price, so an undiscounted orb showed
  $79.99 struck against $32.99 -- correct, that is MSRP -- while the
  bundle-discounted orb beside it showed $32.99 struck against $16.50. Two
  identical products, two different meanings for the same struck figure, and
  the discounted line quietly hid the fact that it is $79.99 off MSRP.

  Every line now strikes MSRP x quantity, so the struck number means the same
  thing on every row and the pill's percentage is the arithmetic between the
  two figures a shopper can actually see.

  Lines are matched to cart items by IDENTITY -- the variant id the row itself
  carries, falling back to the product handle -- and never by position, because
  the drawer re-renders out of cart order after an add. See groupsOf below. The
  FREE GIFT pill is left alone: it is a label, not a percentage.

  (D) ONE ROW PER PRODUCT, AND WHY THE QUANTITY USED TO SKIP.

  Shopify's Buy 2 Get 1 does not discount a line, it SPLITS one: three orbs
  become a line of 2 charged and a line of 1 free. The drawer rendered both as
  ordinary rows, each with its own +/- stepper, and that is the whole bug.

  Measured, three orbs in the cart, pressing MINUS on the row displaying 2:
  the cart correctly went from 3 to 2 -- and that row still displayed 2, because
  the unit Shopify took away was the free one on the OTHER row. The shopper
  pressed minus and the number they were looking at did not move. Pressing PLUS
  repeatedly walked the rows through 2 / 2+1 / 1+2+1 / 1+2+2: rows appearing,
  disappearing and changing places under the finger, with the price on each one
  changing every time the split was re-cut.

  Nothing there is fixable by adjusting a stepper, because the premise is wrong:
  two numbers were being shown for one product, and Shopify -- not the shopper --
  decides how the total is divided between them.

  So the PAID rows for one variant are merged into one. The first carries the
  group: the paid quantity, the sum of the paid line prices, MSRP times that
  quantity struck through, and one honest percentage. Extra paid rows are hidden.

  THE FREE ROW STAYS ON SCREEN, on its own line, by request -- and hiding it was
  a mistake worth naming. Merging it away removed the evidence for what the offer
  had done: the shopper pressed + and, when the new unit became the free one,
  nothing they could see moved. With the free row visible that press has an
  answer -- the free line is what changed. It renders MSRP struck, nothing
  charged, and the word FREE rather than a percentage.

  Its stepper is removed. How many units are free is Shopify's decision, not the
  shopper's, so a control there would argue with the offer. The paid row keeps
  the only stepper and moves the product's whole total.

  THE STEPPER IS INTERCEPTED ONLY ON MERGED ROWS. +/- sets the product's TOTAL
  through a single /cart/update.js that puts the whole quantity on the first line
  and zeroes the others; Shopify then re-cuts the split however it likes, which
  is invisible because the display is rebuilt from the cart either way. A product
  on a single line is not touched at all -- the theme's own stepper keeps working
  exactly as before, so this can only affect the rows that were broken.

  Everything shown is derived from cart.js on every pass, so if the drawer's own
  markup is momentarily stale the figures still converge on the truth.
{%- endcomment -%}
<style>
  /* Backstop for any pill rendered without the inline styles. */
  #cart .pg-free-tag,#cart .pg-save-chip,
  body.template-cart .pg-free-tag,body.template-cart .pg-save-chip{
    display:inline-flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;text-align:center;
    /* same geometry as STYLE below, so a pill painted before the stamp lands is
       already the right width instead of resizing under the shopper */
    width:fit-content;max-width:100%;min-width:0;
    padding:6px 7px 6px 7px;letter-spacing:normal;word-spacing:normal;column-gap:0;text-indent:0
  }

  /* ONE ROW PER PRODUCT -- see (D) in the note above. The extra rows Shopify's
     bundle split creates are hidden rather than removed: the drawer rebuilds
     itself constantly and re-adding removed nodes is the fight that made the
     upsell rows swap places, while a class costs nothing and is re-applied on
     every pass. */
  #cart .l4ca > li.pg-merged-away,
  body.template-cart .l4ca > li.pg-merged-away{display:none !important}

  /* THE FREE ORB GETS ITS OWN LINE, by request -- and it is not editable. Its
     quantity is decided by Shopify's Buy 2 Get 1, not by the shopper, so a
     stepper on it would be a control that argues with the offer. */
  #cart .l4ca > li.pg-free-line .pg-qbtn,
  #cart .l4ca > li.pg-free-line input[type=number],
  body.template-cart .l4ca > li.pg-free-line .pg-qbtn,
  body.template-cart .l4ca > li.pg-free-line input[type=number]{display:none !important}

  /* THE ONE PILL IN THE CART THAT DID NOT HUG ITS TEXT.
     This note is inserted as a sibling of the SAVED chip, and the drawer makes
     that parent a GRID (`#cart .l4ca > li section{display:grid;...}`) which gives
     the chip an explicit grid-area and justify-self:start. The note got neither,
     so it was auto-placed into an implicit row with justify-self:auto -- i.e.
     stretch -- and a stretched grid item with width:auto fills its column. Its
     inline-flex was blockified by grid layout, so inline shrink-wrapping never
     applied either. Measured: 168.8px on a 390px phone and 229.3px on the wider
     desktop drawer, for 111.5px of letters -- it got LONGER as the drawer got
     wider, which is the giveaway that it was tracking the column and not the
     text. Beside a SAVED chip that hugs at 81.8px it read as a long empty purple
     bar across the line.
     width/justify-self give it back its own width; grid-column puts it on a full
     row of its own rather than an odd implicit cell; and the tracking and padding
     now match the chip -- letter-spacing applies after the final glyph too, which
     is the same trailing gap the chip was fixed for. 168.8px -> 118.9px. */
  #cart .pg-free-note,
  body.template-cart .pg-free-note{
    display:inline-flex;align-items:center;
    width:fit-content;max-width:100%;min-width:0;
    /* grid-column-start, not just `1 / -1`. The row's grid declares no explicit
       columns (grid-template-columns computes to `none`), and in that grid `-1`
       resolves back to line 1, so `1 / -1` collapsed and the plaque was
       auto-placed into whatever implicit cell was free -- measured on a phone,
       the price column, where it pushed the + button across the price and hung
       off the right edge of the card. A start line of 1 is unambiguous in both
       kinds of grid: the plaque takes column 1 of the next free row. */
    justify-self:start;grid-column-start:1;grid-column-end:-1;
    margin-top:4px;padding:4px 7px;border-radius:999px;
    background:rgba(139,68,190,.14);
    border:1px solid rgba(168,120,214,.4);
    color:#6B3E93;
    font-size:11.5px;font-weight:800;
    letter-spacing:normal;word-spacing:normal;text-indent:0;
    text-transform:uppercase;
    white-space:normal;overflow-wrap:anywhere;
  }
</style>
<script>
(function(){
  if (window.pgChips) return; window.pgChips = 1;

  /* .nc-lsave-off is the drawer's OWN saved-percent pill, in the price column of
     the same cart line -- pg-unlock has a rule for it, so it is a distinct node.
     It was never in this selector, so it kept the drawer's 12px-a-side padding
     and .05em tracking while the chip three columns away was narrowed to 7px and
     normal: the same words, "SAVED 74%", rendering at two different lengths on
     one line. Adding it is free and idempotent -- if it ever turns out to be the
     same node as .pg-save-chip the extra selector matches the same element and
     changes nothing. */
  var SEL = '.pg-free-tag, .pg-save-chip, .nc-lsave-off';
  /* THE PILL HUGS ITS TEXT. It was `padding:7px 15px` with `letter-spacing:.05em`
     and a matching `text-indent`, which is 30px of purple either side of a ~80px
     word plus a tracked space trailing the % sign -- the badge read as a wide
     slab with the number floating in the middle of it.

     Measured in Chromium against the drawer's own grid: the pill hugs its glyphs
     exactly -- 0px of slack on either side -- so nothing is stretching it and
     there is no layout bug to find. It was simply BUILT wide: 18px of padding
     and .05em of tracking around a 13px word, 96.7px of purple for 79.3px of
     letters. The width therefore comes off the things that actually hold it.
     Padding to a symmetric 7px, size back to the 12px the theme drew it at, and
     tracking off altogether -- 81.8px for 67.8px of letters.

     letter-spacing goes to `normal` rather than a smaller number because it
     applies AFTER the final glyph as well, so any non-zero value leaves a gap
     trailing the % sign. Earlier passes tried to balance that (indent by the
     same amount, then trim padding-right by it) and both kept the gap while
     adding arithmetic; removing the tracking removes the problem, and
     text-indent goes with it. word-spacing and column-gap are pinned for the
     same reason: neither was ever reset, so an inherited value on either could
     widen the pill at the space in "SAVED 55%" without appearing in any rule
     here. */
  var STYLE = {
    'display': 'inline-flex',
    'align-items': 'center',
    'justify-content': 'center',
    'text-align': 'center',
    'font-size': '12px',
    'line-height': '1',
    /* 6px, not 4px. Height is font-size x line-height plus this padding and
       nothing else, so this is the only lever on the badge's aspect that does
       not touch its width: 20px tall -> 24px tall, for free. */
    'padding-top': '6px',
    'padding-bottom': '6px',
    'padding-left': '7px',
    'padding-right': '7px',
    'letter-spacing': 'normal',
    'word-spacing': 'normal',
    'column-gap': '0',
    'text-indent': '0',
    'border-radius': '999px',
    'width': 'fit-content',
    'max-width': '100%',
    'min-width': '0',
    'flex': 'none'
  };

  /* THE STAMP RE-ASSERTS, AND IT DOES NOT WAIT. Two faults, one fix.
     pg-drawer builds every pill with its OWN inline !important geometry
     (font-size:12px, line-height:1.5, padding:4px 12px), and inline !important
     can only be beaten by inline !important -- this stamp. It used to mark each
     element with dataset.pgChip and skip it forever after, so any pill the
     drawer rebuilt in place kept the wide slab; and it only ran on a 300ms
     interval, so even a fresh pill wore that slab for up to a third of a second
     and visibly snapped narrower under the shopper. The guard is now the
     rendered value rather than a one-shot flag, and an observer runs it in the
     same task the pill is inserted in. setProperty with an unchanged value is
     free, so re-checking costs nothing. */
  /* THE GUARD MUST COVER WHAT IT IS GUARDING. It used to test two of the
     nineteen properties the loop writes -- paddingLeft and letterSpacing -- which
     meant any other writer landing a single inline !important declaration on one
     of the other seventeen left both guarded values untouched, so every later
     stamp returned immediately and the pill kept the foreign value for the life
     of the page. Six of the seventeen change the width. Measured with the real
     stamp running: `width:100%` -> 276px permanent, `min-width:140px` -> 140px
     permanent, `font-size:13px` -> 87.5px permanent, `padding-right:12px` ->
     86.8px permanent, none of them ever corrected. The guard now covers every
     width-bearing property, so the only thing it can skip is a pill that is
     already right. */
  function isStamped(el){
    var st = el.style;
    return st.paddingLeft === '7px' && st.paddingRight === '7px'
        && st.letterSpacing === 'normal' && st.wordSpacing === 'normal'
        && st.textIndent === '0px' && st.fontSize === '12px'
        && st.width === 'fit-content' && st.minWidth === '0px'
        && st.maxWidth === '100%' && st.columnGap === '0px';
  }
  var stamping = false;
  function stamp(){
    if (stamping) return;
    stamping = true;
    try {
      document.querySelectorAll(SEL).forEach(function(el){
        if (isStamped(el)) return;
        for (var k in STYLE) el.style.setProperty(k, STYLE[k], 'important');
      });
    } finally { stamping = false; }
  }

  if (window.MutationObserver){
    /* childList AND style attributes. It used to be childList only, on the
       grounds that watching style would make the stamp re-trigger itself -- true,
       but the cost of not watching was worse: pg-drawer restyles these pills
       inline (padding:4px 12px, letter-spacing:.05em) after every drawer rebuild,
       and that is an ATTRIBUTE change, invisible to a childList observer. So the
       correction was left to the 300ms interval and the shopper watched the pill
       sit wide and then snap narrower under their eyes on every cart change.
       Self-triggering is handled properly instead: `stamping` blocks re-entry,
       and the stamp is idempotent -- a second pass over an already-stamped pill
       writes nothing, so the cascade is one extra callback that does no work. */
    new MutationObserver(function(){ stamp(); })
      .observe(document.body, {childList: true, subtree: true});

    /* ATTRIBUTES, BUT ONLY WHERE PILLS LIVE. Watching style on the whole body
       would fire for every unrelated inline write in the theme -- and there are
       several a second, from four different sections' polling loops -- for a set
       of about five pills that all live in one subtree. Scoped to the drawer and
       the cart page's form, it fires for pg-drawer's pill restyle (the write
       that actually matters) and almost nothing else. Attached lazily because
       #cart may not exist yet, and re-checked until it does. */
    var attrSeen = [];
    (function watchAttrs(){
      ['#cart', 'body.template-cart .form-cart'].forEach(function(sel){
        var el = document.querySelector(sel);
        if (!el || attrSeen.indexOf(el) > -1) return;
        attrSeen.push(el);
        try {
          new MutationObserver(function(){ stamp(); })
            .observe(el, {subtree: true, attributes: true, attributeFilter: ['style', 'class']});
        } catch(e){}
      });
      if (attrSeen.length < 2) setTimeout(watchAttrs, 500);
    })();
  }

  /* ---------------- the honest percentage ---------------- */

  var CAP = null;   /* variant id -> compare-at in cents (falls back to price) */

  fetch('/products.json?limit=50').then(function(r){ return r.json(); }).then(function(j){
    CAP = {};
    (j.products || []).forEach(function(p){
      (p.variants || []).forEach(function(v){
        var price = Math.round(parseFloat(v.price || 0) * 100);
        var cap = Math.round(parseFloat(v.compare_at_price || 0) * 100);
        CAP[v.id] = cap > price ? cap : price;
      });
    });
  }).catch(function(){});

  function money(c){ return '$' + (c / 100).toFixed(2); }

  /* WHAT THE LINE ACTUALLY COST, whichever field the discount lands in.
     Shopify puts LINE-level discounts in final_line_price and ORDER-level ones
     (the standing Extra 10% off entire order) in line_price -- and which of the
     two is the smaller depends on which kind of discount is running, so reading
     either one alone under-reports the saving the moment the other applies.
     The lower of the two is the amount actually charged for the line. */
  function paidOf(it){
    var a = typeof it.line_price === 'number' ? it.line_price : null;
    var b = typeof it.final_line_price === 'number' ? it.final_line_price : null;
    if (a === null) return b;
    if (b === null) return a;
    return Math.min(a, b);
  }

  function lines(){
    return document.querySelectorAll('#cart .l4ca:not(.in-panel) > li, body.template-cart .form-cart .l4ca:not(.in-panel) > li');
  }

  /* THE ROW SAYS WHICH VARIANT IT IS. This used to pair rows to cart lines by
     HANDLE and DOM ORDER, and that is the bug behind "prices all over the place
     after an upsell, fine again after a refresh".

     Buy 2 Get 1 splits one product across several lines of the SAME handle, so
     handle alone cannot tell them apart and the pairing fell through to
     position. The drawer re-renders asynchronously after an add: measured
     earlier in this cart, pressing + left the free row rendered ABOVE the paid
     row while the cart still listed paid first. Whenever those two orders
     disagree, every figure written below lands on the wrong row -- one line's
     price on another line's row, the wrong row marked FREE, the deal plaque on
     some rows and not others. A reload rebuilds the drawer in cart order, the
     two agree again, and it all looks fixed.

     The row itself knows its variant: pg-cart-variants renders a <select> whose
     value is the variant id, and the title link carries ?variant= too. That is
     an identity rather than a position, so re-ordering cannot break it. */
  function variantOf(li){
    var sel = li.querySelector('select.pg-vsel, select[data-pg-var]');
    if (sel && /^\d+$/.test(sel.value || '')) return sel.value;
    var a = li.querySelector('a[href*="variant="]');
    if (a){
      var m = (a.getAttribute('href') || '').match(/variant=(\d+)/);
      if (m) return m[1];
    }
    return null;
  }

  function handleOf(li){
    var a = li.querySelector('a[href*="/products/"]');
    if (!a) return null;
    var m = (a.getAttribute('href') || '').match(/\/products\/([^/?#]+)/);
    return m ? m[1] : null;
  }

  /* Bucket rows and cart lines by the same key, and REFUSE THE PASS if the two
     sides do not correspond exactly. Variant id is the precise key, but it is
     only readable off a row when the theme has drawn a variant <select> or a
     ?variant= link, and neither is guaranteed -- a single-variant product gets
     no select at all. So the product handle is kept as a second key: coarser,
     but every row carries a product link, and a handle bucket still separates
     one product's lines from another's, which is all the split below needs.
     If neither key identifies every row, this returns null and the pass is
     skipped -- mid-rebuild the drawer can hold three rows while the cart holds
     two, and the honest thing then is to leave the theme's own render alone
     rather than write confident wrong numbers over it. That refusal is the
     whole fix: the old code always produced a pairing, even when there was no
     correct one to produce. */
  function bucket(rows, items, rowKey, itemKey){
    var rowsBy = {}, order = [], i, v;
    for (i = 0; i < rows.length; i++){
      v = rowKey(rows[i]);
      if (!v) return null;
      if (!rowsBy[v]){ rowsBy[v] = []; order.push(v); }
      rowsBy[v].push(rows[i]);
    }
    var itemsBy = {};
    for (i = 0; i < items.length; i++){
      v = itemKey(items[i]);
      if (!v) return null;
      (itemsBy[v] = itemsBy[v] || []).push(items[i]);
    }
    if (order.length !== Object.keys(itemsBy).length) return null;
    for (i = 0; i < order.length; i++){
      v = order[i];
      if (!itemsBy[v] || itemsBy[v].length !== rowsBy[v].length) return null;
    }
    return {order: order, rowsBy: rowsBy, itemsBy: itemsBy};
  }

  function groupsOf(cart){
    var rows = Array.prototype.slice.call(lines());
    var items = cart.items || [];
    if (!rows.length || !items.length) return null;

    return bucket(rows, items, variantOf, function(it){ return String(it.variant_id); })
        || bucket(rows, items, handleOf,  function(it){ return it.handle || null; });
  }

  function setText(el, t){ if (el && el.textContent.trim() !== t) el.textContent = t; }

  function repct(){
    if (!CAP) return;
    var els = lines();
    if (!els.length) return;

    fetch('/cart.js').then(function(r){ return r.json(); }).then(function(cart){
      var G = groupsOf(cart);
      if (!G) return;      /* drawer and cart disagree -- see groupsOf above */

      function isFreeItem(it){ return it.line_price === 0; }
      function setWas(li, cents){
        li.querySelectorAll('s, del, [class*="was"], [class*="old"], [class*="compare"]').forEach(function(el){
          if (el.closest('.pg-save-chip') || el.closest('.pg-free-tag')) return;
          var t = (el.textContent || '').trim();
          if (!/^\$[\d.,]+$/.test(t)) return;
          setText(el, money(cents));
        });
      }
      /* LAST, not next to the chip. The chip's parent is `.cols`, which is
         `display:contents` -- its children are laid out by the row's grid, so a
         plaque inserted beside the chip competes for a cell in the middle of the
         row. Appended to the grid container itself it is placed after every
         other item, which with grid-column-start:1 means a fresh row underneath
         them. */
      function noteOn(li, text){
        var note = li.querySelector('.pg-free-note');
        if (!note){
          note = document.createElement('span');
          note.className = 'pg-free-note';
        }
        var host = li.querySelector('section') || li;
        if (note.parentNode !== host || note !== host.lastElementChild) host.appendChild(note);
        setText(note, text);
      }
      function noteOff(li){
        var n = li.querySelector('.pg-free-note');
        if (n && n.parentNode) n.parentNode.removeChild(n);
      }

      /* WHICH DEAL THIS LINE IS ON. The orb ladder is not one offer, it is
         several, and they do not behave alike -- measured on the live cart:
             4 orbs  PokeOrb - Buy 2 Get 1 Free   free line, 1 @ $0.00
             6 orbs  PokeOrb - 6 Pack $137.20     ONE line, 6 @ $20.58, none free
             9 orbs  PokeOrb - Buy 5 Get 3 Free   free line, 3 @ $0.00
         So the plaque has to name the rung the shopper actually landed on. The
         standing order-wide 10% is skipped: it is on every line and is not a
         rung. The "PokeOrb - " prefix goes because the row already names the
         product. */
      function dealTitleOf(item){
        var ds = item.discounts || [], i, t, amt;
        for (i = 0; i < ds.length; i++){
          t = ds[i].title || ds[i].description || '';
          amt = ds[i].amount != null ? ds[i].amount : (ds[i].total_allocated_amount || 0);
          if (t && !/entire order/i.test(t) && amt > 0) return t;
        }
        for (i = 0; i < ds.length; i++){
          t = ds[i].title || ds[i].description || '';
          if (t && !/entire order/i.test(t)) return t;
        }
        return '';
      }
      /* The DEAL, not the price. Shopify names this rung "PokeOrb - 6 Pack
         $137.20", and printing that in the plaque put a third figure on a row
         that already shows a struck price and a charged one -- three numbers,
         one of them the bundle total rather than anything to do with this line.
         The product prefix and any trailing amount both come off, leaving the
         offer: 6 PACK, BUY 5 GET 3 FREE. */
      function dealLabel(t){
        return String(t || '')
          .replace(/^[^-]*-\s*/, '')
          .replace(/\s*[-–:]?\s*\$[\d.,]+\s*$/, '')
          .trim().toUpperCase();
      }
      function dealOfItems(its){
        for (var i = 0; i < its.length; i++){
          var t = dealTitleOf(its[i]);
          if (t) return t;
        }
        return '';
      }

      G.order.forEach(function(v){
        var rws = G.rowsBy[v], its = G.itemsBy[v];

        var freeItems = its.filter(isFreeItem);
        var paidItems = its.filter(function(it){ return !isFreeItem(it); });

        var paidQty = 0, paidSum = 0, freeQty = 0;
        paidItems.forEach(function(it){ paidQty += it.quantity; paidSum += (paidOf(it) || 0); });
        freeItems.forEach(function(it){ freeQty += it.quantity; });

        var cap0 = CAP[its[0].variant_id] || 0;
        var label = dealLabel(dealOfItems(its));

        /* ROLES BY COUNT, NOT BY IDENTITY. Every row in this group is the same
           variant -- the same orb -- so which physical row is called the paid one
           and which the free one is arbitrary. What matters is that the counts
           match, and groupsOf has already refused the pass if they do not. The
           last N rows take the N free lines. Nothing is read back off a row, so
           the order the drawer happens to render them in cannot produce a wrong
           figure any more. */
        var cut = rws.length - freeItems.length;
        var paidRows = rws.slice(0, cut);
        var freeRows = rws.slice(cut);

        paidRows.forEach(function(li, i){
          li.classList.remove('pg-free-line');
          if (i === 0) li.classList.remove('pg-merged-away');
          else li.classList.add('pg-merged-away');
        });

        var head = paidRows[0];
        if (head){
          head.setAttribute('data-pg-keys', its.map(function(it){ return it.key; }).join('|'));
          head.setAttribute('data-pg-total', String(paidQty + freeQty));

          var q = head.querySelector('input[type=number]');
          if (q && q.value !== String(paidQty)) q.value = String(paidQty);

          setText(head.querySelector('.nc-lsave-now'), money(paidSum));
          if (cap0) setWas(head, cap0 * paidQty);

          var chip = head.querySelector('.pg-save-chip');
          var base = cap0 * paidQty;
          if (chip && base > 0){
            var pct = Math.round((base - paidSum) / base * 100);
            if (pct >= 1) setText(chip, '-' + pct + '%');
          }

          /* a rung that yields free units is labelled on the free row; one that
             does not -- the 6 Pack is a fixed bundle PRICE -- is labelled here,
             so the per-unit drop is never unexplained */
          if (freeQty > 0) noteOff(head);
          else if (label) noteOn(head, label);
          else noteOff(head);
        }

        freeRows.forEach(function(li, i){
          var it = freeItems[i];
          li.classList.remove('pg-merged-away');
          li.classList.add('pg-free-line');
          li.removeAttribute('data-pg-keys');
          li.removeAttribute('data-pg-total');

          var fq = it.quantity;
          var fi = li.querySelector('input[type=number]');
          if (fi && fi.value !== String(fq)) fi.value = String(fq);

          setText(li.querySelector('.nc-lsave-now'), money(0));
          if (cap0) setWas(li, cap0 * fq);

          var fchip = li.querySelector('.pg-save-chip');
          if (fchip) setText(fchip, 'FREE');
          noteOn(li, label || (fq > 1 ? (fq + ' FREE') : 'FREE'));
        });
      });
    }).catch(function(){});
  }

  /* ---- +/- on a merged row sets the product's TOTAL -- see (D) above ---- */
  var stepping = false;
  function rowOf(el){
    return el.closest('#cart .l4ca > li, body.template-cart .l4ca > li');
  }
  document.addEventListener('click', function(e){
    if (!e.target.closest) return;
    var btn = e.target.closest('.pg-qbtn');
    if (!btn) return;
    var li = rowOf(btn);
    if (!li) return;

    var keys = (li.getAttribute('data-pg-keys') || '').split('|').filter(Boolean);
    /* a product on ONE line is none of our business: the theme's own stepper
       already handles it correctly, and intercepting it could only break it */
    if (keys.length < 2) return;

    var t = (btn.textContent || '').trim();
    var delta = t === '+' ? 1 : (t === '-' || t === '−') ? -1 : 0;
    if (!delta) return;

    e.preventDefault();
    e.stopImmediatePropagation();
    if (stepping) return;
    stepping = true;

    var total = parseInt(li.getAttribute('data-pg-total'), 10) || 0;
    var target = Math.max(0, total + delta);

    /* the whole quantity onto the first line, nothing on the rest, in ONE call.
       Shopify re-cuts the free/paid split afterwards however it wants; that is
       invisible because the row is rebuilt from the cart either way. */
    var updates = {};
    keys.forEach(function(k, i){ updates[k] = i === 0 ? target : 0; });

    fetch('/cart/update.js', {
      method: 'POST',
      headers: {'Content-Type': 'application/json'},
      credentials: 'same-origin',
      body: JSON.stringify({updates: updates})
    }).then(function(){
      if (window.pgDrawerRefresh){
        try { return Promise.resolve(window.pgDrawerRefresh()); } catch(err){}
      }
    }).catch(function(){}).then(function(){
      stepping = false;
      repct();
      [200, 700, 1600].forEach(function(ms){ setTimeout(repct, ms); });
    });
  }, true);

  /* WHY THIS IS NOT A 700ms POLL ANY MORE. repct() fetches /cart.js, so polling
     it every 700ms -- alongside pg-cart-total doing the same -- meant several
     requests a second for a cart that changes a handful of times a visit.
     Shopify throttles that endpoint: under test the responses started coming
     back unparseable, and because repct() bails when the fetch fails the row
     simply froze on its last good figures. A cart display that stops updating
     because it asked too often is worse than one that asks less.

     It now runs when the drawer actually changes, which is the only time the
     numbers can move, with a slow interval left as a backstop. stamp() touches
     no network -- it only re-applies inline styles to new pills -- so it stays
     frequent. */
  var pending = false;
  function soon(){
    if (pending) return;
    pending = true;
    setTimeout(function(){ pending = false; repct(); }, 80);
  }
  if (window.MutationObserver){
    var drawer = document.getElementById('cart');
    if (drawer) new MutationObserver(soon).observe(drawer, {childList: true, subtree: true});
  }

  setInterval(stamp, 300);
  setInterval(repct, 2500);
  stamp();
  repct();
})();
</script>
{% schema %}
{"name": "PG Chips", "settings": []}
{% endschema %}
